# What NOT to upload to Vercel.
#
# IMPORTANT — this file REPLACES .gitignore for upload filtering. Vercel
# falls back to .gitignore only when no .vercelignore exists, so everything
# .gitignore was doing for deploys has to be repeated here. That is why
# node_modules, dist and .env appear below even though .gitignore already
# lists them: leaving them out would start uploading them.
#
# .env is the one that actually matters. A `vercel` CLI deploy uploads the
# working directory as-is, so an unignored .env would be shipped to the
# build and Vite would read it INSTEAD OF the values set in Project
# Settings → Environment Variables — silently deploying whatever this
# laptop happens to have configured. (Git-based deploys are safe either
# way, since the repo never contains .env.)
#
# Note this does not change the built OUTPUT. Nothing here ever reached
# dist/: the renderer talks to the desktop layer through window.gymOS,
# which preload injects at runtime, so no file under src/ imports anything
# from electron/. This is about upload size and deploy time, not about
# stopping desktop code from leaking into the web bundle — that was never
# happening.

# --- must repeat from .gitignore (see above) ---
node_modules
dist
dist-ssr
.env
.env.*.local

# --- desktop app: source is kept in git for versioning, but Vercel has no
#     use for any of it (npm run build only reads src/, public/,
#     index.html, vite.config.js) ---
electron
build
release

# --- one-off operational scripts: seeding plans, the multi-branch
#     migration, and the logo/installer-art generators. Run by hand, never
#     part of a build. ---
scripts

# --- Firebase project config: deployed with firebase-tools, not Vercel ---
firebase.json
.firebaserc
firestore.rules
firestore.indexes.json
storage.rules

# --- docs and local tooling state ---
BUILD.md
.claude
.vscode
.idea

# --- logs / os cruft ---
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
